A||||11||Black88GT||College Ramblings||||8||
Z||000000||Black88GT||10-13-2000||01:08 AM||||This college thing is B.S.  I would prefer going to a Catholic college (smaller teacher/student ratio, religous studies), and you can't even touch one with a 20 foot pole for under 20 grand a year.  The state colleges have 20 billion people in them and I would have to commute, and most of the local ones don't even offer the major I want.  Then you have all these counselors telling you that you can just get loans to pay for college.  Well I don't feel like paying back $60,000 - $80,000 in loans after I graduate college.  I thought the point of college was to get a good job and make money.  Well how can you make good money when you graduate with all that debt!  Maybe in the long run it will work out but it seems like a lot of B.S. you have to go through before that.<P>Starting to touch upon the application process this month.  I took a look at a couple online ones.  All I have to say is, OH MY GOD.  It is going to take hours upon hours to fill all of them out.  I have never seen anything so long in my entire life.  Can't wait to write an essay on top of the other 50 thousand things I have to do for college.  Then in college you have to take all of those stupid courses that have nothing to do with what your majoring in!  That makes a lot of sense.  What the hell is high school for? Then they say "you should visit the college before you apply".  Well most middle class people like myself don't exactly have a private jet to go flying around the country to visit a college you probably will not even go to.    <P><BR>Sorry, I guess the stress is getting to me! Its so late and I have searching colleges on line night and day for the past week.<P><BR>AHHHHHH!!!!!!!!!<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||8
Z||000001||Cheever2||10-13-2000||01:23 AM||||I picked the college where I knew the most girls.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Didn't do so hot with the academics.  But I was glad it was only 4 hours from home... I usually brought my dirty laundry to Mom to wash    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>The week I got strep pretty much ensured that I got academic probation.  So the moral is: women weaken the brain; don't go too far from the nest; and don't get sick.<P>My folks are so proud of me.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||10-13-2000||02:54 AM||||Well I am going for my PhD now, and looking back, the entire undergraduate thing was way too easy.  Now that I am teaching undergraduates I know what my professors said about me, because I am saying the same things now.  <BR>If you are going to go to graduate school, your undergrad school isnt that important.  You just need a good enough school to get you into where you want to go for grad school.<BR>I went to a podunk state school, and got accepted into Carnegie Melon, Drexel, Penn State, Univ of Montana, Univ or Michigan, and Univ of Nev Las Vegas.  The only school I applied to and didnt get in was Univ of Wisconsin Madison. <P>Some of the above schools have a really good name, and I was surprised they accepted someone like me from podunk college, and not having a 4.0 GPA and not doing EXCELLENT in my subject area graduate school SAT (called a GRE).   <p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||10-13-2000||03:36 AM||||Try going to a local college for the 1st couple of years to get the general studies out of the way.  Then you can transfer to a Catholic college (more money, longer drive?) for the last 2 years for the classes in your major.  That's what I planned on doing except I got stupid and dropped out after a year.  Now I get to work 80 hours a week for 20k a year.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||10-13-2000||08:25 AM||||College costs have increased more rapidly than inflation and I feel your pain.  However when I went to college after high school I had something called a job (at $1.25/hr)which I worked to have money for my car and school.  In the summers I had two jobs to have money for my car and school.  (not much time for partying)I did not have the luxury to have dear old mom to take care of me other than to provide a roof over my head, nor was there all this free money that the government gives to those who feel entitled to a free ride.<P>Welcome to the real world..<P>And I love all those buggers who take the loans, then default on them. (my tax dollars hard at work)<P>I had lots of incentive to go on to school after high school.  It was called Viet-nam.  But after I graduated, I got to the orient anyway.<P>Life is tough and it is expensive.  I bet you wished you hit the books a little harder in high school so you could qualify for all those scholarships that go to those who meet the criteria.<P>You'll love even more after collage (making all the big bucks) when you see 30-40% of your income going out in the tax bill paying for the next generation wanting a free ride.<P>||||reg||2
Z||000005||Black88GT||10-13-2000||09:20 AM||||Old Lar, to pay for my Fiero, insurance, gas, and anything else I could possibly want (not need according to my parents) I have 3 part time jobs.  <P>I don't think you meant your comments in an offensive way, however I do take offense to some of them.<P>For example, you say "I had something called a job", somehow implying that I have never worked before.  I actually started working when I was 11, under the table of course because it is illegal to be on payroll under 13 or 14 in my state.  I got my first "real" job freshmen year and still have it in addition to the other two (not enough hours).<P>During this summer, I was averaging 50 hours per week at an average of $6.00 per.  Not bad however when you take into account lunch everday (5 or 6 bucks) gas once a week (15-20 bucks) insurance every month (170 per) that isn't too much.<P>During the school year I cut my hours about in half to 20-25 per week.  I try to work mainly on the weekends but that doesn't leave much time to do anything else.  After school I do work study (catholic h.s.) for an hour and a half 3 days per week, then I usually go home for an hour then go to one of my jobs from 4-9 or 5-9 or something like that.<P>I do agree with you though on the "free ride" thing.  Growing up for 14 years in baltimore city, I witnessed first hand a lot of that in action while my parents were busting their butts working 60 hours a week so I didn't have to go to the poor excuse for schools named Baltimore City public schools.<P>You are right also about my H.S. grades.  Junior year killed me because I was working too much and not taking enough time on my school work.<P>I am assuming you are older (over 50) and I can understand how you would think that about teens.  Thats popular with older people.  But not everyone is the same...  <P> <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tigger||10-13-2000||10:03 AM||||Yea, it sucks, I just shelled out tuition for my fall term yesterday.  It's crazy, I could have bought a nice Fiero with that money.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Awe well.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Don't feel pressured to go to a "big" college.  I'm kind of biased because I go to a community college... but go to a smaller college.  Usually the classes are smaller and more one on one with the instructor.  How much you want to learn is all up to you in the end anyway.<P>I know friends that are off to the big universities to get away from home, party, make it to class, party, and are only digging themselves deeper in student loans.  Yea, they may have a better chance to get hired because of the name on their degree, but I I'm not sure if that's as important.  I think the biggest thing business's are looking for is that you furthered your education, struggled thru it, did the best you could, and completed it.<P>I just wanted to add that I go to college full time and work full time.  It's not easy... but I'm not going into debt either.  Evening classes suck, but if you don't have a family, g/f (wish I did   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ), wife, or a life (that would be nice too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) they are great because you can work normal hours during the day.  Best thing is you can catch up on TV sitcoms when you watch the summer re-runs.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">             <BR>    <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-13-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000007||Black88GT||10-13-2000||03:03 PM||||Tigger, thats why I want to go to a Catholic school because they are smaller class sizes and faculty to student ratios.  I am looking at St. Francis in PA.  However I would have to live on campus, and living in a 5x5 cubicle with a tiny prison like bed with crappy food isn't exactly my idea of an ideal living space, especially if I am paying $20,000+ tuition.  I will probably go to visit though before I decide for sure.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||10-13-2000||04:32 PM||||I know what you mean..<P>I'm going to a fairly large college right now, and some of my classes have up to 40 students.. However other programs have hundreds of students in a class! Humber College is supposed to be pretty good for technical courses, and I'm learning quite a bit. But I plan on looking for a job related to cars immediatly after college anyway, and I don't really want to get a job <BR><B>directly</B> doing what I'm studying now.<BR>(I'm studying electro-mechanical engineering, and the program is focused on industrial controls and automation... aka robotics.)<P>Some stuff is pretty fun though, I was working in the shop for about 2 hours this afternoon and learned how to use a metal-cutting bandsaw, a vertical milling machine, and a tool grinder... and I only got slightly burned once.. hehe.. a guy in my class was showing me how to do a specific angle on the grinder, and after doing one cut, he handed me the work piece without dipping it in the water bucket first... I dropped it pretty quickly. no harm done. hehe..<P>Also, I definitely hear ya about on-campus housing.. I live in the residence at my school. the room is 8.5' x 11.5'... including the desk and closet! I have a bed, a desk chair, a small fridge and a metal shelf unit. I don't have room for anything else.... and don't even get me started on the residence cafeteria food.. I've been living on salads, cheese & crackers, pasta and I usually go to Pizza Pizza for lunch.. the school food court also has mr. sub, harveys, and a few other places.. they had a taco bell last year too, but it's gone now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>anyway, good luck selecting a school!<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000009||frontal lobe||10-13-2000||06:45 PM||||I didn't have parents who could help me with college tuition so it was all on my tab. So I went to the local community college for the first 2 years while living at home (so my parents did help).  Everything completely transfered to the U. of Iowa's pharmacy school.  In no way was I behind in preparation compared to the people that had started at Iowa the first 2 years.<P>I lived in small, crappy places for the next 4 years (pharmacy school was 5 years back then, and then I got right into medical school) and then with my sister the next 3 years.<P>Now instead of a 10x12 foot room I have lived in a 3,000 square foot house for the last 12 years and can live in it as long as I want.<P>I'm not bragging at all.  Here's my point.  Suck it up for 4 to 6 years to be able to live it up for 40 to 60 years.<P>And go cheap on the first 2 years as long as you know the school you want to go to will accept the credits.  The first 2 years are about the same anywhere (academically).||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||10-13-2000||10:19 PM||||Jeff.. I didn't mean to insult you.  But as you can see by some of the other posts that we all go through lifes struggles when we start out.  And many people think they are owed a free college education. (Actually I think college costs have risen so much is because the free education syndrome and that Uncle Sam will pay for it because I'm entitled as with medical costs, insurance pays, who cares).  As long as some people feel that as long as someone else is footing the bill, costs don't matter and cost will rise until it hurts, but costs/prices don't go down.<P>When you have to go out and work your butt off to get the money for what ever you want, you really appreciate it even more.<P>I remember back in my youth making $36 a week and needing a transmission overhaul for my 59 Plymouth, and needing an engine rebuild, a carburator rebuild, water pump, universal joints, generator, voltage regulator, battery, new guages, wheel hubs etc, similiar to all the things we do on the aging Fiero and wondering where would the money come from for college.  <P>With the added incentive if you're not in school, you'll be sloggen through the jungle.<P>Consider this just Ramlings on Life.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fformula88||10-13-2000||11:06 PM||||Catholic Colleges aren't all that great in my opinion. After spending 4 years, about 60K and getting and graduating from Canisius... a fine Jesuit institution here in Buffalo, I must say those big state schools cannotbe that much worse.  Sure it was a good school, but the State Univ. is about 1/2 to 1/3 the price.  <P>If you want the smaller class sizes... try a 2 year school and then transfer for your major work.  Its cheaper and you'll get the catholic school degree.||||reg||1
